  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1230 PM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nThe dry cold front continues to push across the local area on \nWednesday evening into the overnight period. Light northerly winds \nwill be in place through the period with some gusts of 15 to 20 mph \nover the northern Hill Country and areas along and east of Highway \n77. With some light winds and clear skies on Wednesday night, can't \nrule out some patchy to areas of fog along the Rio Grande and \nsouthern Edwards Plateau. Overnight lows in the upper 40s over some \nlocations of the southern Edwards Plateau with the rest of the area \nin the 50s.\n\nThursday and Friday are looking excellent with highs in the 70s and \nsunny skies with cooler overnight lows in the upper 40s and low 50s. \n\nA pattern chance is forecast for the upcoming weekend as an eastern \nPacific short wave enters northern Mexico and then moves across \nSouth Central Texas. Just like this past weekend, things start off \nthe Rio Grande and then spreading eastward with time. There is a 30 \nto 60 percent rain chances through the period with highs in the \n60s and 70s. Rain chances come to an end from west to east on \nSunday afternoon through the evening. However, rain chances come \nright back as another cold front pushes across the local area \nearly next week. However, low confidence on this at this time.",
